From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: KLINGON: Anybody have a provenance?

> I saw one of Okrand's books on Klingon a while back.  It was quite interesting,
> but utterly failed to answer my biggest question --- which is, what human
> languages did Okrand model it on?

I don't know, but my impression is that he didn't actually model it
on anything.  To some extent he made an effort to make it conspicuously
unlike real human languages--for example, making the word order
Object-Verb-Subject, which is so rare that until a few years ago
it was thought to not occur, and maybe to not be possible, in real
languages.  (Turns out there are one or two Indian languages in
Brazil that seem to have it).

But Okrand's background is in native languges of California; he
did his dissertation on Mutsun, an extinct language originally
spoken around Monterey Bay.  How much Mutsun or California Indian
in general might have percolated through into Klingon I don't
know.


