Date: Sun, 27 Jun 93 01:59:15 -0700
From: freeman@MasPar.COM (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: MEDIA: B: _Jurassic_Park_

Stanton McCandlish made a number of interesting criticisms of the
animal behavior and other biology portrayed in _Jurassic_Park_; I
agree with most of what he says, but since I am interested in animal
behavior I thought it might be fun to play devil's advocate (voice of
_Velociraptor_?)  for the story, and see if I could come up with
justifications for what was shown.

> It is never explained how they created extinct plants (remember the leaf,
> right before the protagonists saw their 1st dino?). [...]
> ...I guess the idea is that they used preserved plant material and
> directly cloned it.  Is this believable, from what we now know
> about cloning?

I suspect it is no more unbelievable than the idea of cloning
dinosaurs from preserved blood.  We don't know much about cloning from
DNA samples, but it is amusing to note that cloning higher lifeforms
is a very old human technology, and probably most of you have done it
at one time or another, perhaps with a geranium (which I would call a
higher lifeform because it is a multicelled eukaryote).

> It suffers from what I call the "Alien Syndrome":

Yes.  But just for fun, let's see how fast I can talk ...

> Would a veloceraptor attack a T.rex?

That might depend on how strong the bonds were within the _Velociraptor_
pack.  Naturalist Adolph Murie, in _The_Wolves_of_Mount_McKinley_ (1944,
recently reprinted) has documented several instances of wolf packs 
attacking grizzly bears when the latter came too close to the wolf dens.
The _Velociraptor_ in question also looked as if it were undertaking a
rather effective attack; it looked to me as if it was trying to slash
at throat blood vessels.  Considering that _T._rex_'s short "arms"
didn't reach the area where the _Velociraptor_ was, that might have
worked.

> Would a veloceraptor go after humans despite being caged by them, shot
> at, etc etc, in a totally foreign environment (the inside of a
> building?)

I think the biggest problem here is that the _Velociraptors_ had been
fed an enormous meal less than 24 hours before, and would not likely
have been hungry.  We know that one _Velociraptor_ was shot and likely
killed, at the start of the movie, but it seems unlikely that any of
the others had any reason to know what a gun was, or to think that
humans were dangerous.  Furthermore, they had only just escaped from
captivity into the "human paddock" of the park, which likely contained
no other edible creatures much larger than an appetizer.  If the
_Velociraptors_ had been hungry, it would make great sense for them
to go after the obvious large instances of dinner in residence.
It is also not clear that the insides of buildings were foreign to
them; they had to be raised somewhere before being put into the
paddock, what do you bet they were in inside enclosures.  They might
even associate the interiors of buildings with security.

> I can see the dinos going after humans if they were hungry AND
> reasonably assured of success.

I agree about hunger, but as for success, they were doing pretty well!
Did everybody notice that during all of _Jurassic_Park_, no human
manages to kill a dinosaur?

> the predators we know tend to get away from humans [...]
> and are scared of fire and guns and loud noises [...]

Fear of noise may be instinctive.  Fear of guns _per_se_ -- not merely
as noisemakers -- surely must be cultural (I mean the predators'
cultures), for firearms have not been around for timespans likely
required for evolution to occur.  I don't have any data for predators
at hand, but S. Eltringham, in _The_Ecology_and_Conservation_of_Large_
_African_Mammals_ (1979) cites a result suggesting that rhinoceroses
have a cultural adaptation to hunting techniques: In areas where they
have most commonly been hunted with short-range weapons (not
firearms), they charge when humans appear, so that hunters scatter
before they can bring their weapons to bear.  In areas where firearms
have been long used, they run away from humans.  And in those few
areas where rhinos have not been hunted (possibly none at present,
but the data were from 1963), they are neither fearful nor hostile
toward humans.

Fear of humans is evidently at least partly cultural, because it is
often not seen in animal populations with little contact with humans.
L. David Mech and Jim Brandenburg studied arctic wolves on Ellesmere
Island a few years ago, and found the wolves curious and not fearful;
after a few weeks they would approach within meters.  This is *not*
typical of mainland wolf populations!  Mark and Delia Owens, in
_Cry_of_the_Kalihari_ (1984 -- a *wonderful* book) , had similar
experiences with African lions, brown hyenas and African wild dogs,
and also reported that one of the first brown hyenas they saw
initiated physical contact with them of its own free will on the
second occasion that they saw it.  (Delia( Owens squatted down and the
animal -- a 100-pound carnivore -- came up and put its nose in her
hair.)

As for fear of fire, Owens and Owens (_op._cit._) report that some of
the local lions occasionally would come and curl up on the opposite
side of the campfire from them, a few meters away from it, outside
their tent at their research site.

> I won't even bring up the cage attack sequence at the start, that was
> just silly.

There was a frightened animal in a small, strange place, being moved
around, with strange motions, noises and smells.  It was indeed silly
that the cage was that poorly handled, but I find it plausible that
the critter within would be frantic and dangerous. 

> And besides, the dinos didn't seem the least interested in EATING
> people just hunting them down and killing them.

I had the impression that the _T._rex_ ate the lawyer (albeit leaving
scraps), I thought the "spitter" was last seen completing its
traditional predatory pattern on the nerdly crook, and I noticed that
only one piece of the hapless fellow who first went to turn the power
on was found ...

> Another thing is the wires. [...]

I agree that this is a problem.  However, it depends on the past
history of the specific animals.  If they had ever been confined by
conventional fencing, they would have some experience with non-shocking
walls.  And if they had learned the association of (for example) 
transformer hum with electrified fencing, they might have been
quick to investigate.  And it's not clear that what the _Velociraptors_
cut through was intended as physical security via strength; perhaps it
was just aluminum wire to carry the high voltage.

> Another really dumb thing is the lack of [back-up systems].

Yes!

> Why frog DNA?  At least lizard DNA would've matched a little better.

Yes.

> Would a T.rex really make THAT much noise and shaking-around as it walked?
> Could it really move that fast?

There was an article in _Scientific_American_ on the last subject
within the last few years; the author suggested that a human running
at top speed might have been able to outrun a _T._rex_.  You can try
it first ...

> there was no reason in hell to clone carnivorous dinosaurs.

I'd pay more to see _T._rex_ than to see _J._random_veggiesaurus_.

