> I've never met a working physicist who was willing to commit one way 
> or the other.

Steven Weinberg says this in his _Dreams of a Final Theory_, where he
comes down in favour of many-worlds.  Most serious theoretical
physicists are very unhappy with Copenhagen.  In quantum gravity,
according to one poll I saw, most workers favour many-worlds, Hawking
and Weinberg being the two most famous.

As a final plug for DoaFT, let me recommend it to anyone interested in
the relation between science and philosophy (or physics and metaphysics,
if you prefer).  It goes into detail, amongst other things [*], on the
relation between retrodiction and prediction their use in deriving and
judging new theories.  In contrast with the simplistic and increasingly
out-moded Popperian view that a new theory must make a falsifiable
_prediction_.  Weinberg argues that logically and historically this is
not and has not been the case.  Theories are judged by their simplicity
and fitness with _pre-existing_ data.  Only later, usually after partial
or nearly-full acceptance, are predictions searched for to bash in the
final nails in the lid of acceptance.  No-one builds a supercollider
unless they have a pretty good idea of what they're looking for (eg a
Higgs particle).

General relativity, the theory par excellence, was not accepted on the
basis of the 1919 eclipse (the evidence from which was highly
questionable, Weinberg reports), contrary to popular mythology.  General
relativity was (and is) accepted because of its logical structure and
_re-interpretation_ of existing data on the orbit of Mercury.  Evidence
that may have contradicted Einstein was ignored by the eclipse
experimenters.  This is always the danger, Weinberg explains, with
predictions borne out by experiment.  The experimenter knows what she's
testing for and systematically routes about eliminating experimental
error and bias until data fits the theory.  With the re-interpretation
of existing data by a theory (a _retrodiction_) no such blinkering takes
place.

Looking further back, Newton's theory of universal gravitation was re-
interpreting planetary data that Kepler had already theorised about in
his orbital laws.  The successful Newtonian prediction of Halley's comet
return (ie laws derived from planetary behaviour, applied to comets) was
not verified until Newton and Halley's death and well after the
acceptance of the theory (in England, at least).

In quantum mechanics the retrodiction of many-worlds is the collapse of
the wavefunction relative to the observer.  In Copenhagen this effect
has to be introduced at the level of a postulate (eechh!), adding
greatly to the complexity of the theory (and also making it vitalistic).

By contrast many-worlds is _much_ more compact and simpler theory,
wherein the peculiar behaviour is derived from a basic set of elegant
postulates/axioms.  On complexity grounds alone many-worlds is the
winner.  Dr David Deutsch's proposed experiments with reversible memory
units coupled with intelligence are hardly necessary to vindicate many-
worlds.  I will make a prediction here.  By the time we become capable
conducting the Deutsch experiments many-worlds will be standard.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk
                                      AS member (21/3/93)

[*] PS one of the other things Weinberg mentions, inter alia, is the
non-chaotic nature of the universe.  Only classical systems, with their
non-linear feedback loops, exhibit chaotic behaviour.  Quantum systems
are always linear and theorefore non-chaotic.  Small perturbations of
initial conditions only lead to small divergences to final conditions. 
It is more correct, Weinberg continues, to speak of _quasi-chaotic_
systems: that is systems that would exhibit behaviour _if_ they were
classical.  Changes below a certain threshold do not cause exponential
diverge.  The beat of butterfly wing in Brazil may cause a tornado in
Texas, but the pandemonium down at the planck level doesn't perturb the
universe in the same manner.  Interesting stuff.

