Subject: AIT VirtSem: "Information"

Hal Finney writes:
> his reasoning was that
> deterministic processes cannot create new information in the AIT sense.
> [eg Conway's Life creates no new information]

This sounds controversial if we confuse terms of art.
Information theory uses the word "information[1]" as a measure of 
data uncertainty.  To most layfolk, and in the field of information 
systems, "information[2]" is a qualitative estimate of utility (as opposed 
to "data", which can be useful or useless, and is measured in bits).
These are vastly different meanings.   For example, a random bit string
has a maximal info[1] content, but often no info[2] content.  
In AIT "information[3]" is the bit length of the smallest 
algorithm to which a string can be compressed (to some standard, 
such as Lisp s-expressions or the encoding of a Turing machine).

The vast bulk of traditional mathematics, eg as defined by Russel & 
Whitehead in _Principia Mathematica_, also contains very little 
info[3].  R&W derive most 1900's-era math knowledge from just a few 
axioms, eg the axioms of the predicate calculus.  By the incompleteness 
theorem not _all_ of math is so compressible, and by some measures
of string-compression only an insignificant fraction of what could
be defined as "math" is compressible, but the vast bulk of math
as used in the early 20th century (and probably also today) is
in principle extremely compressible.   Similary pi, e, and many other
infinitely expandable irrationals with H_trad=1 are highly
compressible.   

We should quickly realize that there is a 
tradeoff between the degree to which an algorithm (such as
a mathematical system) has been compressed, and its efficiency
at solving practical problems.  We usually can't predict the emergent
patterns in Life, other than by running the algorithm.  
If we had to rederive math from the predicate calculus every time
we wanted to make change at the grocery store, we would be
in sad straights indeed -- but in principle, given very large
amounts of very cheap computing power, we could do this.
In fact modern computers do something reminiscent of this,
computing arithmetic vastly faster than a human via circuits
that implement Boole's Laws of Thought (a simplified form
of the predicate calculus).

In turn, much of our scientific and engineering knowledge
can be compressed into mathematics, the remainder being 
observational data to fit to the mathematics.  Thus, most
of the vast scientific knowledge and engineering skill can
be compressed into (a) observations, containing a vast amount
of info[3], and (b) a tiny number of axioms,
containing a small amount of info[3], but expandable into
a rich array of models that can in turn be used to stochastically
(imprecisely) compress observational data into useful forms.
The utility of math comes from expanding the axioms to a point
where they become efficient models of our observations that
help us achieve our goals.  Good math is neither too simple
nor too complex, but just right for the job.  "Just right"
often translates to orders of magnitude difference in computing
speed.

Furthermore as we have seen, the halting problem itself can be 
compressed to a small algorithm that generates and samples
Turing Machines, if we allow infinite computing time.
We can achieve a performance of eliminating O(n^3) TMs per n trials 
with GA and presumably GP.  In finite time a vast number of
halting problems can be solved (and indeed are daily solved
by both software engineers and their compilers).

Measuring info[1] with the function H_trad() and info[3] with
the function H_AIT(), H_trad(Life,t+n) > H_trad(Life,t)
for almost all n.  In AIT terms
	H_trad (Life,n) >> H_AIT (Life) = H_trad (Life, 0)
for large n.  I define the "expansion coefficient" of an algorithm as
X(a) = H_trad(a)/H_AIT(a).  The algorithm with highest X(a)
is probably a high-entropy psuedorandom number generator created 
by Koza's genetic program (_Genetic Programming_, pg. 404).

The uncertainty principle in physics might be restated to say that
quantum mechanical processes have an X(a) of infinity,
and could (if tapped perfectly "fairly", as in fair coins)
generate a number stream with H_trad(a)=1, something a Turing machine
can only approach. 

On the ohter hand, there may exist an algorithm that compresses quantum 
uncertainty, just like there exist an iterative expression that 
compresses the H_trad=1 digits of pi.   If so, we haven't discovered 
it yet.  If not, we have infinite supply of info[3] that is
practically free (merely the cost of setting up a fair quantum 
process and observing it fairly).  However, as we have seen
info[3] is not the same thing as info[2], useful
data.   Information[2] comes about when the axioms of mathematics
are expanded "just right", so that they optimally model our
observations and thereby help us achieve our goals.  

Let us define "information[4]" as info[2] without the subjective
aspects of values and goals, but including the notion of balancing
model complexity with observational complexity.  I hypothesize
that info[4] is maximized when info[3]+info[1] is minimzed.
Or perhaps, some other f(info[3],info[1] with a positive slope.

