Subject: Self-replication is easy (but not _that_ easy :-)

Dani Eder's design is getting more interesting as he adds detail.
Besides the photovoltaics, motor parts, and electronics, I see several 
other loops to close, many of them having to do with assembly 
automation in a broad sense, some having to do with gritty
details that become important for self-rep:

Power distribution (esp. insulating wires & power conditioning
	for motors, computers, CCDs, etc.)
Designing flexible, many-DOF robots outs of hexrod parts
	(flexible mills and robots usually contain control wires, 
	bearings and other very precise lubricated joints, and motors
	or hydraulics which must be assembled in an
	intricate geometry) that can carry out all necessary
	operations (eg transporting dirt without spilling,
	mirror line-of-sight, wire routing, cleaning, etc.)
Wire drawing machine
Manufacture insulating wire coating (can this be done without plastic?)
Drill bits (these must be much harder than aluminum, 
	eg diamond, boron nitride)
Positioning & aligning hex parts to drill or place mounting points
Design of mounting points, considering the wide variety of assembly 
  configurations for robots, mills, wire drawer, solar concentrator,
  etc.
Valves, pumps, & seals for vacuum chamber (to keep reactants in or air out)
Heat transfer wall for reactor (light -> ?? -> refractory -> reactants)
loading, unloading, & cleaning reactor: access ports to enable,
	robots to perform (including scrubbing muck off refractory)
making sure motors, CCDs, reflector surfaces, and other sensitive 
	equipment stays clean operating near reactor
coating refractory on walls, define process, what machine(s)
	needed, what if any material(s) needed to make coating stick
claw & shovel attachments: forming, cutting, mounting, etc.
CCDs & CPUs (cheat & bring these from Earth :-)
Coolant to dissipate heat at drill/part interface (for many
	mills, mass of coolant used exceeds mass of parts produced)
Cleaning & disposing of mill chips
Lubrication (for motors, pumps, valves, gears, bearings, etc.)
Lubrication (for drill/part interface if operating in vacuum)
making all the new machines needed to do this stuff, making
	all the machines needed to make _those_, etc.
[more details I've probably missed]

I strongly suspect that the assembly design problems, while complex, 
can be solved by breeding 3D shape grammars with genetic programming,
using fitness functions that measure allosteric (mutual shape fit)
relationships, Von Mises stress and heat flow (using large-grain 
finite elements and wide engineering margins since these are not
important in most assembly cases), and Newtonian physics to
simulate specific tasks (eg robot DOF, reach, and center of
gravity, solar concentrator alignment, DOF, and line-of-sight,
etc.)  I enthusiastically welcome suggestions for software that
might perform mechanical engineering/physics modelling of 3D
parts (and related material & surface information),
or strategies/designs for creating such software.

Designing a self-rep factory is kind of like one of those stereotypical
comedy routines; everytime we close one loop a bunch more spring open.  The 
details are very important, or at least what is considered a "detail" for
this task is very different than traditional systems analysis, where 
we assume a vast array of parts available "off the shelf".  It's
a complex problem, but I think its lack of solution (so far)
is more due to being off the beaten path of traditional factory
design than due to unprecedented complexity.  Dani's giving it a 
good crack!  Keep us posted.

